{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Config schema + layered-merge core + capability descriptor (provider-neutral)\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nThe reconciler's rebar↔Jira mappings are hardcoded literals in\n`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_family/value_maps.py`,\n`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/config.py`, and\n`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/inbound_fields.py`. Before any axis can become\nconfig-driven, the seam needs a provider-neutral foundation: a config schema, a layered\noverlay/merge, a fail-closed validation framework, and a capability descriptor that\ndeclares which axes a target has. This is the walking skeleton every other child in the\nepic depends on. It ships with **no axis wired yet** — it stands up the machinery and\nproves it, and S2–S5 move each axis onto it.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd a provider-neutral module (new file\n`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/mapping_config.py`) that:\n\n1. **Parses** the mapping block as a **reserved config section**, not as new keys on the\n   typed `JiraConfig`. The core coercer (`_config_schema.py`) coerces `[tool.rebar.jira]`\n   into `JiraConfig` (declared fields `url`/`user`/`project`/`allow_insecure`) and\n   `_warn_unknown` warns — or hard-errors under `REBAR_CONFIG_UNKNOWN_KEYS=error` — on any\n   leftover key, so mapping keys **cannot** hang off `[tool.rebar.jira]`. Instead add a new\n   reserved section name `mapping` to `_RESERVED_SECTIONS`\n   (`src/rebar/_config_schema.py:633`, today `{\"llm\", \"snapshot\"}`) so the core parser\n   recognises `[tool.rebar.mapping]` without coercing or warning, and read it raw via\n   `rebar.config.read_reserved_section(\"mapping\")` (`src/rebar/config.py:597`) — the exact\n   pattern the optional `llm.*` layer uses. Shape: a `default` block and a `projects.<KEY>`\n   table, each carrying axis sub-tables (`status_map`, `type_map`, `link_map`,\n   `priority_map`, `create_defaults`) plus discovered-vocabulary lists (`statuses`,\n   `issue_types`, `link_types`, a `hierarchy` level map). Absent section ⇒ empty overlay\n   (today's behavior). `read_reserved_section` does a shallow user↔project file merge; this\n   module owns the deep per-key merge (below), reusing `_deep_merge` where cross-file depth\n   is needed.\n2. **Resolves** an effective mapping for a given project key by deep per-key merge:\n   built-in default (injected by the caller — the adapter's constants) ← `default` block ←\n   `projects.<KEY>`. Per-key means an overlay `status_map` entry overrides only that\n   status; unnamed keys inherit.\n3. **Validates** fail-closed at load: a mapped value must appear in the project's declared\n   vocabulary set when one is declared (e.g. every `status_map` value ∈ `statuses`); a\n   value referencing an axis the capability descriptor marks absent is rejected; malformed\n   blocks raise. The raised type is a new `MappingConfigError` subclassing the existing\n   `rebar._config_coercion.ConfigError` (`src/rebar/_config_coercion.py:20`), so it fails\n   closed on the same load path as every other config error. Validation is **offline** — it\n   reads only config, never the network.\n4. Defines a **`Capability` descriptor** dataclass declaring which mapping **axes** the\n   target's vocabulary has (`has_types`, `has_transitions`, `has_hierarchy`,\n   `has_link_types`, `has_priorities`). This is deliberately distinct from — and does not\n   replace — the backend **role/behaviour** Protocols in `_backend.py`\n   (`@runtime_checkable` `SupportsLinks`/`SupportsComments`, cc77 hasattr-narrowing): those\n   answer \"does this backend **object implement** this operation?\"; the `Capability`\n   descriptor answers \"does this target's **vocabulary** contain this mapping axis?\" (data,\n   not behaviour). Where they overlap (link support), the descriptor is derived from /\n   consistent with the Protocol, not a second source of truth.\n\nThe module imports nothing from the Jira adapter (provider-neutral); the adapter injects\nits built-in defaults and capability values. This respects the vendor-adapter seam's\none-way dependency direction — concrete backends import the neutral core, never the\nreverse — documented in **`docs/adr/0083-reconciler-vendor-adapter-seam.md`** (the seam\nADR; a bare \"ADR 0035\" is ambiguous — three files carry that number — so cite the\nfilename), as amended by `docs/adr/0055-jira-family-sub-seam.md`.\n\nRelationship to the existing per-project surface: `.bridge_state/projects.json`\n(`projects_store.py`, ADR 0097) binds a rebar store to a Jira project **identity** (which\nJira key a local ticket belongs to). `[tool.rebar.mapping].projects.<KEY>` declares that\nJira key's **vocabulary/semantics** (how statuses/types/links map). They are orthogonal\nconcerns kept in separate homes on purpose; the mapping config is keyed by the same Jira\nproject key `projects.json` already resolves, so the two join on that key without\nduplicating identity data.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_config_schema.py` — add `\"mapping\"` to `_RESERVED_SECTIONS`.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/mapping_config.py` (new) — schema dataclasses,\n  `MappingConfigError`, `load_mapping_config`, `resolve_for_project`, `validate`,\n  `Capability`.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_mapping_config.py` (new).\n- `docs/config.md` — document the `[tool.rebar.mapping]` reserved section shape, the\n  three-layer merge, and its orthogonality to `.bridge_state/projects.json`.\n\n## Testing\n\n- **Reserved-section recognition (unit, real parser):** a `[tool.rebar.mapping]` section\n  is neither warned as unknown nor coerced into `JiraConfig`, and is returned raw by\n  `read_reserved_section(\"mapping\")` — asserts the blocking unknown-key path is avoided,\n  including under `REBAR_CONFIG_UNKNOWN_KEYS=error`.\n- **Deep-merge precedence (unit):** a `projects.REB.status_map` entry wins over\n  `default.status_map` which wins over an injected built-in default, and an unnamed key\n  falls through all three.\n- **Config-effect contrast:** resolve at the built-in default and at a non-default overlay\n  value and assert the effective map differs exactly as specified (guards the\n  read-but-miswired class).\n- **Fail-closed validation (failure path):** a `status_map` value not in the declared\n  `statuses` set raises `MappingConfigError`; a malformed block raises; an axis-absent\n  reference raises — each asserted on the exact type, with the nearest valid config as the\n  negative control (must NOT raise).\n- **Neutrality:** assert `mapping_config` imports no `adapters.jira*` module and contains\n  no Jira value literal (e.g. `\"To Do\"`, `\"Initiative\"`).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `[tool.rebar.mapping]` is registered as a reserved section and read raw via\n  `read_reserved_section`, so its keys do not trip `_warn_unknown` even under\n  `REBAR_CONFIG_UNKNOWN_KEYS=error` — verified by\n  `test_mapping_config.py::test_reserved_section_not_unknown`.\n- [ ] `resolve_for_project` deep-merges per key with precedence built-in ← default ←\n  project; an unnamed key inherits — verified by\n  `test_mapping_config.py::test_perkey_merge_precedence`.\n- [ ] A non-default overlay produces a different effective mapping than the built-in\n  default through the real resolve path — verified by\n  `test_mapping_config.py::test_config_effect_contrast`.\n- [ ] `validate` fails closed offline with `MappingConfigError` on: a value outside the\n  declared vocabulary, a malformed block, and a reference to a capability-absent axis; the\n  nearest valid config does not raise — verified by\n  `test_mapping_config.py::test_validate_fails_closed`.\n- [ ] The core is provider-neutral: `mapping_config` imports no `adapters.jira*` module and\n  carries no Jira value literal — verified by\n  `test_mapping_config.py::test_core_is_provider_neutral`.\n- [ ] `docs/config.md` documents the reserved-section shape, three-layer merge, and its\n  orthogonality to `.bridge_state/projects.json`.\n\nTest tier: **in-process unit** — the mechanism is config parse/merge/validate over real\nfunctions (the real reserved-section parser plus the new pure resolve/validate); no\nexternal boundary is crossed, so a lower tier is impossible and a subprocess/live tier\nwould cross no additional component."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787287708381816001,"uuid":"6f44d03c-11c6-4073-94d6-3474b4a2caac"}